Proxy-Control Identifiability Under Intervention-Induced Measurement Distortion

Version 0.2 — Mathematical Foundations, Conditional Recovery, and Design

ouadi and and SI TEAM 

Abstract

We formalize Proxy-Control Identifiability (PCI) as the problem of identifying a latent causal target when interventions simultaneously modify latent dynamics and the observation mechanism. We consider partially observed systems $X_{t+1} = F_\theta(X_t, U_t) + \eta_t$, $Y_t = h_\phi(X_t, U_{0:t}) + \epsilon_t$, where $X_t$ is latent, $Y_t$ is a proxy, and $U_t$ is an intervention with both therapeutic and diagnostic components. We define intervention-conditioned observational equivalence and a target-pushforward Wasserstein gap $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}$ measuring latent-target disagreement among observationally indistinguishable models. We prove constructively, over a multi-step horizon, that identical intervention-conditioned proxy distributions can coexist with different latent trajectories, so observational equivalence does not imply target equivalence. We further show, via a two-state pathological example with explicit dynamics, that eliminating the direct intervention-to-proxy channel is insufficient: the target-sensitive subspace must be dynamically observable through the intervention-induced observability codistribution. We then formulate a conditional recovery theorem under explicit regularity, channel-separation, persistent-excitation, target-observability, and statistical-consistency assumptions, and prove it in the linear-Gaussian case. The framework separates three logically independent requirements—proxy-channel separation, dynamic excitation, and target-relevant observability—and yields a falsification program and decision criterion for whether an intervention-sensitive proxy can identify a latent causal target.

---

1. Introduction

Many control problems operate on latent states $X_t \in \mathcal{X} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ that cannot be observed directly. The controller relies on measurable proxies $Y_t \in \mathcal{Y} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m$. The difficulty intensifies when the intervention $U_t \in \mathcal{U} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^p$ used to modify $X_t$ also directly modifies the observation mechanism $h_\phi$. In this regime, an observed improvement in $Y_t$ may result either from a genuine change in $X_t$ or from an intervention-induced measurement effect. The inference

\Delta Y > 0 \;\Longrightarrow\; \Delta T(X) < 0

is not generally valid, where $T: \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}^q$ is the target functional.

This work formalizes the problem as Proxy-Control Identifiability (PCI). The central question is not how to control a partially observed system, but when an intervention-sensitive proxy can causally identify a latent target. We separate this question from any particular control architecture, biomarker, or application domain, and treat it as a problem in nonlinear system identification and causal inference.

The contribution is threefold. First, we establish a multi-step structural non-identifiability result: identical intervention-conditioned proxy distributions can coexist with different latent trajectories, so observational equivalence does not imply target equivalence. Second, we show via an explicit two-state counterexample with dynamics that eliminating the direct intervention-to-proxy channel is insufficient for target identification; target-relevant dynamic excitation is additionally required. Third, we formulate a conditional recovery theorem under explicit assumptions and prove it in the linear-Gaussian case, separating channel separation, dynamic excitation, target-relevant observability, and statistical consistency.

