2. Mathematical Model

2.1 Latent dynamics and observation

Let $X_t \in \mathcal{X} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$, $U_t \in \mathcal{U} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^p$, $Y_t \in \mathcal{Y} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m$. The latent dynamics are

X_{t+1} = F_\theta(X_t, U_t) + \eta_t, \qquad \eta_t \sim P_{\eta,\theta},
\tag{1}

and the observation model is

Y_t = h_\phi(X_t, U_{0:t}) + \epsilon_t, \qquad \epsilon_t \sim P_{\epsilon,\phi},
\tag{2}

where $U_{0:t} = (U_0, \ldots, U_t)$ is the intervention history. The dependence on the full history, rather than on $U_t$ alone, is required for the multi-step non-identifiability construction of Section 3 and is natural in biological settings where cumulative intervention exposure affects biomarker readings.

The Jacobian of the observation function decomposes into two conceptually distinct sensitivities:

D_X h_\phi \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times n}, \qquad D_U h_\phi \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times p},
\tag{3}

where $D_U h_\phi$ denotes the sensitivity to the current intervention. The problematic regime is $D_U h_\phi \neq 0$. We refer to the direct pathway $U \to Y$, not mediated by $X$, as Intervention-Induced Proxy Distortion (IIPD).

2.2 Candidate model space

A candidate model is

M = (G, \theta, F_\theta, h_\phi, \Sigma_\eta, \Sigma_\epsilon),
\tag{4}

where $G$ is the causal graph encoding the intervention structure. The admissible model set after observing data $\mathcal{D}_t$ under interventions $U$ is

\mathcal{Q}(\mathcal{D}_t, U) \subseteq \mathcal{M}.
\tag{5}

The set $\mathcal{Q}$ is not assumed to contain only parameter uncertainty; it may contain uncertainty over $G$, $F$, $h$, $\theta$, $\phi$, and the noise covariances.

2.3 Histories and predictive distributions

Define the history $\mathcal{D}_t = \{Y_{0:t}, U_{0:t-1}\}$. For a future intervention sequence $U_{t:T}$, the predictive distribution is $P_M(Y_{t:T} \mid U_{t:T}, \mathcal{D}_t)$, and the latent-target distribution is $P_M(T(X_T) \mid U_{t:T}, \mathcal{D}_t)$.

---

3. Equivalence Relations and the Identifiability Gap

3.1 Intervention-conditioned observational equivalence

Definition 1. Two models $M_i, M_j \in \mathcal{M}$ are intervention-conditioned observationally equivalent under $(\mathcal{D}_t, U_{t:T})$, written $M_i \sim_{\mathcal{D},U} M_j$, if

P_{M_i}(Y_{t:T} \mid U_{t:T}, \mathcal{D}_t) = P_{M_j}(Y_{t:T} \mid U_{t:T}, \mathcal{D}_t)
\tag{6}

as probability measures. Equivalently, whenever the relevant densities exist,

D_{\mathrm{KL}}\left(P_{M_i}(Y_{t:T} \mid U_{t:T}, \mathcal{D}_t) \,\|\, P_{M_j}(Y_{t:T} \mid U_{t:T}, \mathcal{D}_t)\right) = 0.
\tag{7}

The equivalence is causal in the sense that the interventions are $do(U)$-style, and the model class includes the causal graph $G$. Two models with the same predictive distribution but different causal structures are distinguished by $G$.

3.2 Latent-target equivalence

Definition 2. Let $T: \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}^q$ be the target functional. Two models are latent-target equivalent, written $M_i \sim_T M_j$, if

P_{M_i}(T(X_T) \mid U_{t:T}, \mathcal{D}_t) = P_{M_j}(T(X_T) \mid U_{t:T}, \mathcal{D}_t).
\tag{8}

The central question is whether

M_i \sim_{\mathcal{D},U} M_j \;\Longrightarrow\; M_i \sim_T M_j.
\tag{9}

3.3 Proxy-Control Identifiability Gap

Definition 3. Let $T_\# P$ denote the pushforward of a latent-state distribution under $T$. Define the target-pushforward Wasserstein-1 distance

W_1^T(\mu, \nu) = W_1(T_\# \mu, T_\# \nu) = \sup_{f \in \mathrm{Lip}_1(\mathbb{R}^q)} \left| \int f \, d(T_\# \mu) - \int f \, d(T_\# \nu) \right|,
\tag{10}

where $\mathrm{Lip}_1(\mathbb{R}^q)$ is the class of 1-Lipschitz functions on $\mathbb{R}^q$. The Proxy-Control Identifiability Gap over horizon $T$ is

\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}(\mathcal{D}_t, U_{t:T}) = \sup_{\substack{M_i, M_j \in \mathcal{Q}(\mathcal{D}_t, U) \\ M_i \sim_{\mathcal{D},U} M_j}} W_1^T\!\left(P_{M_i}(T(X_T) \mid U_{t:T}, \mathcal{D}_t),\, P_{M_j}(T(X_T) \mid U_{t:T}, \mathcal{D}_t)\right).
\tag{11}

The use of the pushforward metric, rather than the full Wasserstein-1 distance on $\mathcal{X}$, is essential. The critic class is $\{f \circ T : f \in \mathrm{Lip}_1(\mathbb{R}^q)\} \subset \mathrm{Lip}_{\mathrm{Lip}(T)}(\mathcal{X})$, so $W_1^T(\mu, \nu) \leq \mathrm{Lip}(T) \cdot W_1(\mu, \nu)$. The pushforward gap is target-relevant and finite under mild moment conditions; the full-state gap may be infinite and is not the object of interest.

Regularity conditions for $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}$. For $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}$ to be well-defined and finite, we require:

· (C1) Compactness/tightness: $\mathcal{Q}(\mathcal{D}_t, U)$ is compact in a topology making $M \mapsto P_M(T(X_T) \mid U_{t:T}, \mathcal{D}_t)$ continuous.
· (C2) Finite first moments: $\mathbb{E}_{M}[|T(X_T)|] < \infty$ for all $M \in \mathcal{Q}$.
· (C3) Measurable selection: the supremum in (11) is attained, or is replaced by an essential supremum over a $\sigma$-finite measure.

Under (C1)–(C3), $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}$ is finite and the implications

\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} = 0 \;\Longrightarrow\; \text{target identification within } \mathcal{Q},
\tag{12}

\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} > 0 \;\Longrightarrow\; \text{non-identifiability}
\tag{13}

hold.

