4. Structural Non-Identifiability

4.1 Theorem A (multi-step non-identifiability)

Theorem A. Consider the scalar system $X_t, Y_t, U_t \in \mathbb{R}$ with $T(X) = X$. Let the model class be parametrized by $(k, \alpha, \beta, \sigma^2)$ with dynamics

X_{t+1} = X_t - \alpha U_t,
\tag{14}

and observation

Y_t = k X_t + \beta U_t + k\alpha \sum_{s=0}^{t-1} U_s + \epsilon_t, \qquad \epsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2) \text{ i.i.d.}
\tag{15}

Then for any horizon $T \geq 1$ and any intervention sequence $\{U_t\}_{t=0}^{T-1}$, any two models $(k, \alpha_1, \beta, \sigma^2)$ and $(k, \alpha_2, \beta, \sigma^2)$ satisfy

M(k, \alpha_1, \beta, \sigma^2) \sim_{\mathcal{D},U} M(k, \alpha_2, \beta, \sigma^2),
\tag{16}

while

P_{M(k, \alpha_1)}(T(X_T)) = \delta_{X_0 - \alpha_1 \sum_{s=0}^{T-1} U_s},
\tag{17}

P_{M(k, \alpha_2)}(T(X_T)) = \delta_{X_0 - \alpha_2 \sum_{s=0}^{T-1} U_s}.
\tag{18}

Consequently,

\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} \geq |\alpha_1 - \alpha_2| \cdot \left| \sum_{s=0}^{T-1} U_s \right|.
\tag{19}

Proof. The latent trajectory under $M(k, \alpha)$ is

X_t = X_0 - \alpha \sum_{s=0}^{t-1} U_s.
\tag{20}

Substituting into the observation equation:

Y_t = k\left(X_0 - \alpha \sum_{s=0}^{t-1} U_s\right) + \beta U_t + k\alpha \sum_{s=0}^{t-1} U_s + \epsilon_t = k X_0 + \beta U_t + \epsilon_t.
\tag{21}

The $\alpha$-dependence cancels exactly. Therefore the observation distribution

P_{M(k, \alpha)}(Y_{0:T-1} \mid U_{0:T-1}) = \mathcal{N}\!\left(k X_0 \mathbf{1} + \beta U_{0:T-1},\, \sigma^2 I\right)
\tag{22}

is independent of $\alpha$. Hence (16) holds.

The target at time $T$ is $T(X_T) = X_T = X_0 - \alpha \sum_{s=0}^{T-1} U_s$, which depends on $\alpha$. Since both distributions are Dirac, the Wasserstein-1 distance is

W_1^T\!\left(P_{M(k, \alpha_1)}(T(X_T)),\, P_{M(k, \alpha_2)}(T(X_T))\right) = |\alpha_1 - \alpha_2| \cdot \left| \sum_{s=0}^{T-1} U_s \right|.
\tag{23}

Taking the supremum over $\alpha_1 \neq \alpha_2$ in the admissible class gives (19). $\blacksquare$

Remark 1. The construction requires the observation function to depend on the intervention history $U_{0:t}$, not just on $U_t$. This is a strict generalization of the model $h_\phi(X_t, U_t)$ and is natural when cumulative intervention exposure affects the measurement. If the observation function depends only on $U_t$, the multi-step result fails: at $t = 2$, the direct channel cannot cancel the cumulative latent effect without history dependence.

Remark 2. The unbounded-gap claim requires an unbounded admissible model class or intervention domain. For a fixed compact $\mathcal{Q}$ and bounded $\mathcal{U}$, $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}$ remains finite. The rigorous statement is: $D_U h \neq 0$ permits arbitrarily large latent ambiguity only when the admissible model/intervention class permits it.

