5. Channel Separation

5.1 Diagnostic interventions

Write the intervention as

U_t = U_t^{\mathrm{ctrl}} + \delta U_t^{\mathrm{diag}}.
\tag{24}

A diagnostic perturbation should ideally satisfy

|D_U F \, v| \ll |D_U h \, v|,
\tag{25}

meaning that the perturbation strongly affects the observation channel while minimally changing the latent dynamics. Such a direction need not exist; diagnostic decoupling is an existence condition, not an assumption.

5.2 Local diagnostic decoupling ratio

Definition 4. For $v \in T_U \mathcal{U}$ with $|v| = 1$, define

\rho(v; X, U) = \frac{|D_U h_\phi(X, U) \, v|_{\mathcal{Y}}}{|D_U F_\theta(X, U) \, v|_{\mathcal{X}} + \lambda},
\tag{26}

with $\lambda > 0$. Define $\rho^\star(X, U) = \sup_{|v| \leq 1} \rho(v; X, U)$. A direction $v^\star$ is $\rho_{\mathrm{crit}}$-diagnostic if $\rho(v^\star; X, U) \geq \rho_{\mathrm{crit}}$.

If $\rho^\star(X, U) < \rho_{\mathrm{crit}}$, the system is locally non-decouplable under the specified intervention class.

5.3 Limitation

The ratio $\rho$ measures channel separation. It does not establish that the latent state is observable. A direction can strongly perturb $Y$ while carrying no information about a target component of $X$. Diagnostic decoupling $\neq$ latent identifiability. This is why a second condition is required.

