6. Dynamic Excitation and Target-Relevant Observability

6.1 Pathological compensation with dynamics

Consider the two-state system

X_t = (x_{1,t}, x_{2,t}) \in \mathbb{R}^2,
\tag{27}

with dynamics

x_{1,t+1} = x_{1,t} + u_{1,t}, \qquad x_{2,t+1} = x_{2,t} + u_{2,t},
\tag{28}

observation

Y_t = x_{1,t} - x_{2,t} + \beta U_t + \epsilon_t,
\tag{29}

and target

T(X) = x_1 + x_2.
\tag{30}

The observation Jacobian is $dh = (1, -1)^T$, so the unobservable subspace is

\ker(dh) = \mathrm{span}\{(1, 1)^T\}.
\tag{31}

The dynamics preserve this direction: if $\delta X = (\delta, \delta)^T$, then $F(X + \delta X, U) - F(X, U) = \delta X$, so the unobservable direction is invariant. The Lie derivatives of $h$ along $F$ are

L_F^0 h = x_1 - x_2,
\tag{32}

L_F^1 h = (1, -1) \cdot (x_1 + u_1, x_2 + u_2) = x_1 - x_2 + u_1 - u_2,
\tag{33}

and higher Lie derivatives are of the same form, with additional input terms. The observability codistribution is

\mathcal{O}_F(h) = \mathrm{span}\{(1, -1)^T\}
\tag{34}

for all orders. The direction $(1, 1)^T$ is never observable.

Therefore:

· (i) The direct channel can be identified: $\beta$ is estimable from the fast response.
· (ii) The direction $(1, -1)^T$ is observable: $x_1 - x_2$ is estimable.
· (iii) The direction $(1, 1)^T$ is unobservable: $x_1 + x_2$ is not estimable.
· (iv) Consequently, $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} > 0$ despite perfect channel separation.

This invalidates any theorem based solely on the existence of a direction satisfying a large $\rho$.

6.2 Dynamically induced observability

Let the continuous-time analogue be $\dot{X} = F_\theta(X, U)$, with output $Y = h_\phi(X, U)$. The nonlinear observability codistribution is generated by

\mathcal{O}_F(h) = \mathrm{span}\{dh, \, dL_F h, \, dL_F^2 h, \ldots\}.
\tag{35}

For discrete-time systems, the corresponding construction uses successive compositions of the state-transition and observation maps. Diagnostic information may enter an initially unobservable latent direction only after propagation through the dynamics. The relevant object is not $dh$ alone, but the dynamically generated family $\mathcal{O}_F(h)$.

6.3 Target-relevant observability

Full state observability is stronger than necessary when only a target functional is required. Let $T: \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}^q$. The target is locally observable if

\mathrm{span}(dT) \subseteq \mathcal{O}_F(h).
\tag{36}

For scalar $T$, $\mathrm{rank}(dT) = 1$. For complete state identification, $\mathrm{rank}\,\mathcal{O}_F(h) = n$. The target-relevant formulation is preferable because the controller need not reconstruct every latent degree of freedom if only a particular functional matters.

6.4 Intervention-induced observability matrix

Definition 5. Define the local intervention-induced observability sequence

\mathcal{O}_k^{\mathrm{diag}}(v) = D_X(L_F^k h) \cdot D_U F \cdot v, \qquad k = 0, \ldots, K.
\tag{37}

Stacking these terms gives

\mathscr{O}_K(v) = \begin{bmatrix} D_X h \cdot D_U F \, v \\ D_X L_F h \cdot D_U F \, v \\ \vdots \\ D_X L_F^K h \cdot D_U F \, v \end{bmatrix}.
\tag{38}

The exact form must be adapted to the continuous- or discrete-time model and to the regularity assumptions on $F$ and $h$.

6.5 Target-relative rank condition

For a scalar target, a necessary local condition is that $dT$ not remain in an unobservable direction after the diagnostic perturbation:

\mathrm{rank}\,\mathscr{O}_K(v^\star) \geq \mathrm{rank}(dT).
\tag{39}

For vector-valued targets, this scalar rank comparison is insufficient by itself. The stronger requirement is target distinguishability:

\delta X \in \ker \mathscr{O}_K(v^\star) \;\Longrightarrow\; dT(X) \, \delta X = 0.
\tag{40}

Equivalently, the target-sensitive subspace must not be contained in the dynamically unobservable subspace:

\ker \mathscr{O}_K(v^\star) \cap \{\delta X : dT(X) \, \delta X \neq 0\} = \varnothing.
\tag{41}

6.6 Persistent excitation of induced regressors

Definition 6. A matrix sequence $\{\Phi_t\}_{t=0}^{N-1} \subset \mathbb{R}^{d \times p}$ is persistently exciting of order $K$ if there exists $c > 0$ such that for all $N$ sufficiently large,

\sum_{t=0}^{N-1} \Phi_t \Phi_t^T \geq c I_d.
\tag{42}

For the diagnostic experiment, the induced regressor is

\Phi_t = \mathscr{O}_K(v^\star) \, \delta_t,
\tag{43}

where $\delta_t$ is the diagnostic perturbation magnitude. The persistence condition (42) ensures that the diagnostic excitation spans the target-relevant observability directions over the horizon.

Remark 3. Persistent excitation of $\delta U_t v^\star$ does not automatically imply persistent excitation of the induced regressors $\Phi_t$. The PE condition must be imposed on $\Phi_t$ directly, not merely on the input direction.

