7. Conditional Recovery

7.1 Theorem B (conditional proxy-control identifiability recovery)

Theorem B. Let $\mathcal{Q}$ be a model class satisfying:

· (A1) Regularity: $F_\theta$ and $h_\phi$ are $C^\infty$ on a compact admissible set $K \subset \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{U}$.
· (A2) Bounded uncertainty: $\mathcal{Q}$ is compact in a topology making $M \mapsto P_M(T(X_T) \mid U)$ continuous, and $\mathbb{E}_M[|T(X_T)|] < \infty$ for all $M \in \mathcal{Q}$.
· (A3) Diagnostic channel separation: There exists $v^\star \in T_U \mathcal{U}$ with $|v^\star| = 1$ such that $\rho(v^\star) \geq \rho_{\mathrm{crit}} > 0$.
· (A4) Persistent excitation: The induced regressor sequence $\{\Phi_t = \mathscr{O}_K(v^\star) \delta_t\}_{t=0}^{N-1}$ is persistently exciting of order $K + 1$ in the sense of Definition 6.
· (A5) Target-relevant observability: For all $X$ in the admissible set,

\ker \mathscr{O}_K(v^\star) \cap \{\delta X : dT(X) \, \delta X \neq 0\} = \varnothing.
\tag{44}

· (A6) Statistical consistency: The estimator $\mathcal{Q}_N \to \mathcal{Q}^\star$ in probability, where $\mathcal{Q}^\star$ is the set of models consistent with the true data-generating process.

Then

\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}(\mathcal{D}_t \cup \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{diag}}^N, U \cup U_{\mathrm{diag}}^N) \longrightarrow 0 \quad \text{as } N \to \infty,
\tag{45}

provided the true data-generating system belongs to $\mathcal{Q}$ and the identifiability assumptions hold uniformly over $\mathcal{Q}$.

Proof for the linear-Gaussian case. Assume

X_{t+1} = A X_t + B U_t + \eta_t, \qquad \eta_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma_\eta),
\tag{46}

Y_t = C X_t + D U_t + \epsilon_t, \qquad \epsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma_\epsilon),
\tag{47}

with $T(X) = L X$ for $L \in \mathbb{R}^{q \times n}$. The observability matrix is

\mathcal{O}_K = [C; \, C A; \, C A^2; \, \ldots; \, C A^{K-1}].
\tag{48}

The diagnostic perturbation induces the regressor $\Phi_t = \mathcal{O}_K B v^\star \delta_t$.

Under (A5), $\ker \mathcal{O}_K \subseteq \ker L$. Therefore there exists a matrix $M \in \mathbb{R}^{q \times mK}$ such that

L = M \mathcal{O}_K.
\tag{49}

The latent target at time $T$ decomposes as

L X_T = M \mathcal{O}_K X_T = M [C X_T; \, C A X_T; \, \ldots; \, C A^{K-1} X_T].
\tag{50}

Each block $C A^k X_T$ is expressible in terms of the observations $\{Y_t\}$, the inputs $\{U_t\}$, and the noise realizations $\{\epsilon_t\}$ through the recursion

C A^k X_T = Y_{T-k} - D U_{T-k} - \epsilon_{T-k} - \sum_{j=1}^{k-1} C A^{k-1-j} B U_{T-k+j} - (\text{noise terms}).
\tag{51}

Under the cleaned observation $\widetilde{Y}_t = Y_t - D U_t$ (with $D$ estimated under (A3)), the deterministic part of $L X_T$ is a known function of $\{\widetilde{Y}_t, U_t\}$. Therefore, if two models $M_i, M_j$ produce the same distribution of $\{\widetilde{Y}_t, U_t\}$, they produce the same distribution of $L X_T$. Hence

P_{M_i}(L X_T \mid U) = P_{M_j}(L X_T \mid U),
\tag{52}

and $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} = 0$.

The statistical contraction under (A4) and (A6) follows from standard arguments: the persistent excitation ensures that the parameter estimates converge, and the consistency of the estimator ensures that $\mathcal{Q}_N$ contracts to $\mathcal{Q}^\star$. Therefore (45) holds. $\blacksquare$

Proof sketch for the nonlinear case. Under (A1) and (A2), the observability codistribution $\mathcal{O}_F(h)$ has constant rank on the compact admissible set. Under (A5), the target codistribution $dT$ is contained in the dynamically generated observability codistribution, so the target is a function of the observable outputs and inputs. The cleaned observations $\widetilde{Y}_t = Y_t - D_U h \, v^\star \delta_t$ isolate the state-dependent component, and the observability operator $\mathscr{O}_K(v^\star)$ provides a local diffeomorphism between the target-relevant latent directions and the cleaned observation sequence. The persistent excitation condition (A4) ensures that the local diffeomorphism is uniformly invertible over the horizon. The statistical contraction then follows from (A6) and standard consistency arguments for nonlinear system identification. A complete proof requires uniform observability over the compact set, which is guaranteed by the constant-rank condition on $\mathcal{O}_F(h)$. $\blacksquare$

7.2 Interpretation

The theorem does not claim that a large $\rho$ is sufficient. The structural package required for asymptotic contraction is

\text{channel separation} + \text{persistent excitation} + \text{target-relevant observability} + \text{consistent inference}.
\tag{53}

The result separates two problems that are often conflated: estimating the direct proxy effect and identifying the latent target.

